A||||21||geyser||urethane bushings||||1||
Z||000000||geyser||12-07-1999||07:50 PM||||<BR>Okay all you serious suspension nuts, I have always been told to use high durometer rubber bushings for A-arms and other dynamic suspension components, with the exception of sway bars. Using urethane would cancel the needed deflection that the suspension needed to keep the tires flat against the pavement. This comes from years of autocrossing. However, the popular swap for the Fiero is urethane all around. Any comments?||||reg||1
Z||000001||pollock||12-07-1999||08:27 PM||||I disagree.  I think the difference in deflection between a high-durometer rubber and poly-u would be negligible.  Anyway, you shouldn't have to count on bushing flex to keep your tires flat.  that's what the moving suspension parts are for.  bushings are just there to absorb vibration and wear.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eddie||12-07-1999||08:37 PM||||I am note a serious suspension nut but I like the poly over the stock I think they hit alittle to hard.AND YOU DO NOTE need that in a fiero. ||||reg||6
Z||000003||2birds||12-07-1999||09:07 PM||||I have had the poly bushings in my 86 GT for 4 years now, and I have a few comments on them.  <BR>One, they really make the ride rough.  I've tried them with and without my lowering springs, and it doesn't change much.  You feel every single groove, rumble strip, etc  (I think I feel a filling coming loose now). Two, the plus side is I'm not wearing out the insides of my rear tires in 20,000 miles any more.  I think this is because the camber  doesn't change as much, but it could also be the rear anti-sway bar. <BR>Three, I wish I would have put in grease fittings when I had everything apart.  I think this would make the ride more liveable.  The suspension really doesn't give, except for on real sharp bumps. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||12-07-1999||09:34 PM||||There is always a sacrifice in comfort when you go after better handling and vice versa. That's why a Caddy rides nice and smooth, but corners like a lumber wagon.||||reg||1
Z||000005||GREENDANO||12-09-1999||06:06 PM||||Bryan,<BR>  Have you driven a Cadillac lately?  Computer suspensions let them have the best of both worlds.<BR>  I like the road feel you get with the urethane bushings.  Fieros are technically sports cars and should have a sporty ride,  IMO.<BR>  Dan-O<p>[This message has been edited by GREENDANO (edited 12-11-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||12-10-1999||03:54 PM||||I agree with 2birds. I have had the poly bushings in my 88 Formula for 8 months and they made the ride more harsh. And don't talk about the dog bone. Replaced that too and the engine does not move but feel lot more vibration. But at least I would not have to replace any bushings soon :-)||||reg||8
Z||000007||Shiner||12-10-1999||05:18 PM||||I agree with Greendano; this is a sports car here.  They are SUPPOSED to ride rough!  If I wanted a smooth ride, I sure as heck would not have bought a fiero!  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||08:45 PM||||Just an expensive thought, but could you modify the adjustable suspension out of a (C4) Vette?  It had a control to adjust from "sport" to "comfort" (or something).  I wish I had the $$ to try all of these ideas.||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||12-10-1999||10:40 PM||||Not to detract from our wonderful Fieros, but  a car doesn't have to ride like a go-kart to handle well, it's just that the suspension design we are stuck with is the limiter, IMHO.  My Thunderbird Super Coupe would corner better than the Fiero when pushed, and it rode like a Lincoln when it wasn't pushed.  It had adjustable shocks and upper and lower control arms front and rear, not the strut type we are stuck with.  Isn't progress wonderful?||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||12-11-1999||12:38 AM||||I agree that the newer suspensions are better than ever, but a T-bird or Caddy are still too nose heavy to hang with a Fiero in a curve. There are advantages to the mid-engine design, even if we have to put up with slightly outdated suspensions.||||reg||1
Z||000011||william_thorniii||12-11-1999||09:32 PM||||Batboy,<P>It is getting more and more possible that our wonderful cars might be getting out cornered here very shortly.  The Mitsubishi Lancer Evo VI just won an Autocar competition and was named the "Worlds Best Handling Car".  I realize that this is off topic, but as a fwd, front-engined vehicle, it outhandled some entries from the Ferrari and Lotus stables which are famed for their handling.  Not to mention that it only costs around 30G's.  In case anyone is curious it also packs 280 bhp out of a 4. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000012||2birds||12-11-1999||09:49 PM||||Has anyone found any GOOD replacement rubber control arm bushings?  It would be nice if there was a happy medium between kidney busting and sloppy handling. I used the poly because I was afraid the GM parts would be way overpriced, and I didn't think the ride would be so harsh.  I drive this car every day, and it really rides like a go-kart.  The bushings don't spin freely, especially when it's cold.  Train tracks almost knock your fillings out...  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||12-11-1999||11:36 PM||||I just put almost 200 miles on the Batmobile today. I've replaced the front control arm and sway bar bushings with poly ones and used lower front springs. I haven't done the back controls arms yet, but I will eventually. Anyway, this is the most I've driven it since the front end rebuild because it's been apart for one project after another. I really expected it to ride much rougher than it did. Actually, I was quite pleased with the way it rode and handled. A little harsher than before, but acceptable for a weekend sports car.||||reg||1
Z||000014||GREENDANO||12-13-1999||07:09 PM||||2BIRDS,<BR> IRM supposedly had hard rubber suspension bushings at one time.  I don't have their number because their catalog is at work.  Anyone have it?  Urethane is far more econimical and worth the swap.  The ride quality isn't as bad as everyone is making it out to be.  It's firmer, but by no means is it bone jarring.  <BR>  Dan-O||||reg||1
Z||000015||mshill||12-13-1999||07:35 PM||||I have about 1000 miles on my front end with poly bushings (including the swaybar and end-links).  The ride is rougher, but compared to the way it handled and rode previously, I wouldn;t have it any other way.<P>I prefer a slightly rougher ride as opposed to a car that wants to wander all over the road everytime I hit a bump.||||reg||12
Z||000016||Cooter||12-13-1999||09:06 PM||||While everyone is talking about urethane bushings, check out: <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm</A> <BR>the prices are great and the guy, Bruce is really nice. Funny thing he did- Accidently sent me a set of trailing arm bushings for an 88 before I actually got my 88 GT. If I had only kept them another day, I would not have had to order a set for my new car.||||reg||1
Z||000017||GREENDANO||12-13-1999||10:33 PM||||Cooter,<BR>  THE EXACT THING HAPPENED TO ME!!!  UPS just picked them up on Friday.  I ordered front cont. arms, rear cont. arms, and a subframe set.  I didn't get the subframe set, but instead got an '88 rear trailing arm kit.  HMMM?  That's weird.  Anyone else get a "mis-sent" set of '88 bushings?  Supposedly my subframe bushings are on the way.  I've ordered from them before for my GT project and didn't have any trouble. Oh well.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||1
Z||000018||Cooter||12-13-1999||11:10 PM||||That is really weird because the 88 bushings that i got were supposed to be rear cradle bushings for my 85.||||reg||1
Z||000019||RFMike||12-14-1999||07:53 AM||||Cooter is right about the prices.  I have checked out prices everywhere I could find on suspension components.  These guys are the cheapest.  Plus, they send out on time.||||reg||1
Z||000020||GREENDANO||12-14-1999||09:06 PM||||Update,<BR>  I got my subframe bushings today.  They look like they will fit my frame better than the bushings they sent.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>  Dan-O||||reg||7
Z||000021||theogre||12-14-1999||09:12 PM||||I've talked to suspesion resto.... nice guy. Probly the way they are numbered or stacked in the warehouse......<P>Considering he doesn't have a fiero he knows the suspension reasonably well. He also knows there are 2 diferant dogbone designs. I wanted to take some pictures for him when I did my bone, but haven't had the time yet. ((I have one of each bone so he can see the dif.))||||reg||1
